  "description" : "Documentation on the M.A.U.S. military ranks and organizational structure.",
  "icon" : "nicemice_codex_maus_ranks.png",
  
  "contentPages" : [
"^#ff8888;M.A.U.S. Ranks and Roles^reset;

M.A.U.S. is organized explicitly around the idea of overlapping duties performed in parallel. Ranks are awarded on basis of field merit, and are not mutually exclusive.

Higher-ranking personnel are expected to lead from the front, and M.A.U.S. is one of the few military organizations that proactively polices cowardice within its ranks.

^#88CC88;>>",

"^#ff8888;M.A.U.S. Ranks and Roles^reset;

Although specific role titles may vary, M.A.U.S. fleet personnel generally fall into one of eight categories:
- ^red;Staff^reset;
- ^yellow;Cargo & Logistics^reset;
- ^orange;Engineering^reset;
- ^#884488;Mechanized Support^reset;
- Medical
- ^#776655;Quartermastery^reset;
- ^green;Domestication^reset;
- ^cyan;Fleet Captains^reset;

^#88CC88;>>",

"^#ff8888;M.A.U.S. Ranks and Roles^reset;

Staff have generalist roles, and will typically perform maintenance tasks.

Cargo & Logistics specialists handle shipping, loading, and unloading.

Engineers maintain the ship's software-centric systems.

Mechanics maintain the ship's hardware-centric systems.

^#88CC88;>>",

"^#ff8888;M.A.U.S. Ranks and Roles^reset;

Medics specialize in keeping the crew healthy, in and out of combat.

Quartermasters maintain the ship's manifest and inventory.

Domestication staff specialize in neutralizing hostile threats.

Fleet Captains function as negotiators, leaders, and liasons.

^#88CC88;>>",

"^#ff8888;M.A.U.S. Ranks and Roles^reset;

Specializations are usually denoted by uniform color, but many roles are cross-disciplinary in nature.

It's common, for example, for mechanics to have engineering experience and share engine or weapon system maintenance with their engineer peers."

  ],
